Step 3 – Install containerd (All Nodes)
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y containerd

Generate the configuration:

sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd

containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml

Enable Systemd cgroups:

sudo sed -i \
's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' \
/etc/containerd/config.toml

Restart:

sudo systemctl restart containerd
sudo systemctl enable containerd
Step 4 – Install Kubernetes

Install prerequisites:

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl gpg

Create the keyring:

sudo mkdir -p -m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings

Import the Kubernetes signing key:

curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.34/deb/Release.key \
| sudo gpg --dearmor \
-o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg

Create the repository:

echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg] https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.34/deb/ /' \
| sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

Update:

sudo apt update

Install Kubernetes:

sudo apt install -y \
kubelet \
kubeadm \
kubectl

Prevent upgrades:

sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl

Enable kubelet:

sudo systemctl enable kubelet
Step 5 – Initialize the Cluster

On the control plane:

sudo kubeadm init \
--apiserver-advertise-address=192.168.56.10 \
--pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16
Step 6 – Configure kubectl
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube

sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf \
$HOME/.kube/config

sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) \
$HOME/.kube/config

Verify:

kubectl get nodes
Step 7 – Install Flannel
kubectl apply -f \
https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml
Step 8 – Fix Flannel for Vagrant (Important)

By default, Flannel selected the VirtualBox NAT interface (eth0, 10.0.2.15) instead of the Vagrant private interface (eth1, 192.168.56.x), preventing inter-node pod networking and causing the ingress admission webhook to time out.

Verify the interfaces:

ip -br address

Expected:

eth0 10.0.2.15
eth1 192.168.56.x

Patch Flannel to use eth1:

kubectl -n kube-flannel patch daemonset kube-flannel-ds \
--type='json' \
-p='[
{
"op":"add",
"path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/args/-",
"value":"--iface=eth1"
}
]'

Restart Flannel:

kubectl rollout restart daemonset/kube-flannel-ds \
-n kube-flannel

Wait:

kubectl rollout status daemonset/kube-flannel-ds \
-n kube-flannel

Verify:

kubectl get nodes \
-o custom-columns='NODE:.metadata.name,FLANNEL-PUBLIC-IP:.metadata.annotations.flannel\.alpha\.coreos\.com/public-ip'

Expected:

controlplane 192.168.56.10
node01       192.168.56.11
node02       192.168.56.12
Step 9 – Join Worker Nodes

Generate the join command:

kubeadm token create --print-join-command

Run it on node01 and node02.

Verify:

kubectl get nodes -o wide
Step 10 – Install ingress-nginx

Use the bare-metal manifest:

kubectl apply -f \
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/ingress-nginx/main/deploy/static/provider/baremetal/deploy.yaml

Wait:

kubectl rollout status deployment/ingress-nginx-controller \
-n ingress-nginx
Step 11 – Install Cert-Manager
kubectl apply -f \
https://github.com/cert-manager/cert-manager/releases/latest/download/cert-manager.yaml

Verify:

kubectl get pods -n cert-manager
Step 12 – Create the Namespace
kubectl create namespace web
Step 13 – Configure RBAC
Generate a client key:
openssl genrsa -out nginx-user.key 2048
Create a CSR:
openssl req -new \
-key nginx-user.key \
-out nginx-user.csr \
-subj "/CN=nginx-user/O=developers"
Base64-encode the CSR and create a CertificateSigningRequest manifest.
Apply and approve it:
kubectl apply -f nginx-user-csr.yaml
kubectl certificate approve nginx-user
Retrieve the signed certificate:
kubectl get csr nginx-user \
-o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' \
| base64 -d > nginx-user.crt
Create a Role in the web namespace allowing management of Deployments, Services, Pods, ConfigMaps, Secrets, and Ingresses.
Create a RoleBinding that binds the nginx-user to that Role.
Build the nginx-user.conf kubeconfig using kubectl config set-cluster, set-credentials, and set-context.
Verify permissions:
kubectl auth can-i create deployment \
--namespace web \
--kubeconfig=nginx-user.conf

Expected:

yes
Step 14 – Deploy NGINX as the RBAC User

Use the limited user's kubeconfig:

export KUBECONFIG=nginx-user.conf

Deploy:

kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f nginx-service.yaml
kubectl apply -f nginx-ingress.yaml

Verify:

kubectl get all -n web
kubectl get ingress -n web
Step 15 – Validate Access

Determine the NodePort assigned to the ingress controller:

kubectl get svc ingress-nginx-controller -n ingress-nginx

Test with the appropriate NodePort:

curl -H "Host: nginx.local" \
http://192.168.56.10:<HTTP_NODEPORT>
Final Verification
kubectl get nodes shows all three nodes in the Ready state.
kubectl get pods -A shows all system pods healthy.
kubectl auth can-i confirms the nginx-user can deploy only within the web namespace.
NGINX is reachable through the ingress controller.
Flannel advertises the 192.168.56.x addresses, ensuring reliable inter-node pod networking.
The deployment satisfies the take-home requirements for a kubeadm-based cluster, RBAC using certificate authentication, and deployment of NGINX by a non-admin user.
